pull_request.body:
This follow-up applies the boundary and actor-allocation model from the self-improving-systems notes.

The article now:
- removes the unnecessary adversative transition after "Modern coding agents" and presents agents straightforwardly as components of a weight-prompt-code-runtime system;
- keeps the deployed compound system, not the model alone, as the unit of learning and explicitly includes prompt templates and retained prompt state among behavior-determining learning surfaces;
- introduces Commonplace as the live human-agent research instrument and persistent-theory programming agents as the harder prospective testbed;
- states why including the operator does not establish the desired automation result: human-inclusive self-improving membership is easy to obtain;
- holds the human-inclusive boundary fixed and reports each decision-bearing function as human, computational, or joint;
- defines progress as reallocation toward computational supply and the automation endpoint as boundary contraction once humans cease to be a cut set;
- keeps that actor-allocation endpoint separate from coherent modification and warranted unattended operation;
- rewrites the recorded Commonplace episode using the same fixed-boundary profile, explicitly noting that the operator remained a cut set; and
- sharpens the longitudinal study to look for named functions moving from human or joint to computational supply, rather than increased agent output volume.

The new framing follows `self-improving-systems-README.md`, `methodological-and-computational-closure-track-different-changes.md`, and `computationally-directed-self-improvement-is-a-reallocation.md`.
